== Solution 6 (Ratio of 15-75-90 Triangle) ==
Let <imath>\triangle ABC</imath> be an isosceles right triangle with the right angle at vertex <imath>C</imath>. Two rays from <imath>C</imath> trisect <imath>\angle C</imath> into three <imath>30^\circ</imath> angles, intersecting the hypotenuse <imath>AB</imath> at <imath>D</imath> and <imath>E</imath>. 

By symmetry, <imath>\triangle CAD \cong \triangle CBE</imath>. Because <imath>\angle A = 45^\circ</imath> and <imath>\angle ACD = 30^\circ</imath>, the exterior angle theorem gives <imath>\angle CDE = 45^\circ + 30^\circ = 75^\circ</imath>. Similarly, <imath>\angle CED = 75^\circ</imath>, making <imath>\triangle CDE</imath> an isosceles triangle with a vertex angle of <imath>30^\circ</imath>.

Let the equal legs of the isosceles right triangle be <imath>AC = BC = s</imath>. The area of <imath>\triangle ABC</imath> is given as <imath>12.5</imath>, so:
<cmath>\text{Area}(\triangle ABC) = \frac{1}{2}s^2 = 12.5 \implies s^2 = 25 \implies s = 5</cmath>

By the Pythagorean theorem, the hypotenuse <imath>AB</imath> is:
<cmath>AB = \sqrt{s^2 + s^2} = s\sqrt{2} = 5\sqrt{2}</cmath>

Let <imath>CH</imath> be the altitude from <imath>C</imath> to <imath>AB</imath>, and let its length be <imath>x</imath>. Because <imath>\triangle ABC</imath> is an isosceles right triangle, the altitude splits the hypotenuse into two equal halves (<imath>AH = BH = \frac{5\sqrt{2}}{2}</imath>) and creates a <imath>45^\circ</imath>-<imath>45^\circ</imath>-<imath>90^\circ</imath> right triangle <imath>\triangle ACH</imath>. Thus, the altitude is equal to half the hypotenuse:
<cmath>x = CH = \frac{5\sqrt{2}}{2}</cmath>

The altitude <imath>CH</imath> bisects the central <imath>30^\circ</imath> angle <imath>\angle DCE</imath>, splitting <imath>\triangle CDE</imath> into two identical <imath>15^\circ</imath>-<imath>75^\circ</imath>-<imath>90^\circ</imath> right triangles (<imath>\triangle CHD</imath> and <imath>\triangle CHE</imath>) where <imath>\angle DCH = \angle ECH = 15^\circ</imath>. 

From the known side-length ratio of a <imath>15^\circ</imath>-<imath>75^\circ</imath>-<imath>90^\circ</imath> triangle, the ratio of the shorter leg (opposite the <imath>15^\circ</imath> angle) to the longer leg (opposite the <imath>75^\circ</imath> angle) is exactly <imath>2 - \sqrt{3}</imath>. Therefore, the bottom leg <imath>DH</imath> is:
<cmath>DH = CH \times (2 - \sqrt{3}) = x(2 - \sqrt{3})</cmath>

Since <imath>H</imath> is the midpoint of <imath>DE</imath>, the full base of the central triangle is <imath>DE = 2DH = 2x(2 - \sqrt{3})</imath>. The area of <imath>\triangle CDE</imath> can be computed by doubling the area of right triangle <imath>\triangle CHE</imath>:
<cmath>\text{Area}(\triangle CDE) = 2 \times \text{Area}(\triangle CHE) = 2 \times \left( \frac{1}{2} \cdot DH \cdot CH \right) = DH \cdot CH</cmath>

Substituting our expressions into the area formula yields:
<cmath>\text{Area}(\triangle CDE) = [x(2 - \sqrt{3})] \cdot x = x^2(2 - \sqrt{3})</cmath>

Since <imath>x = \frac{5\sqrt{2}}{2}</imath>, we find <imath>x^2 = \frac{25 \cdot 2}{4} = \frac{25}{2}</imath>. Substituting this value gives the final exact area:
<cmath>\text{Area}(\triangle CDE) = \frac{25}{2}(2 - \sqrt{3}) = \frac{50-25\sqrt{3}}{2}</cmath>

Therefore, the final answer is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(D) } \frac{50-25\sqrt{3}}{2}}</imath>

